{
  "cutoff_065": 0.65,
  "title": "Full-tier models (union-trained): production BNPL comparison and union holdout",
  "caption": "Full-tier models (5 union-trained packages: best_quality, 10 bag folds, 2 stack levels), scored from the models' minimum model set (the models the best ensemble depends on; prediction is identical to the full folder). Hurdles carry every head (ev / am25 / am50 / am75 / am80 / am85 / am90 / amstar); amstar tau* maximizes the Full model's own train R2 with its native argmax and is frozen (not refit on holdout or live). Top: last-3-month BNPL production cohort. Acceptance n=142,391 (census: every eligible user, no sampling error for this cohort). Delinquency n=2,997 (census: every eligible user, no sampling error for this cohort) (matured CaaS book of the last 180 days). The shaded bands and the \"(95% CI)\" figures treat each cohort as one draw from ongoing traffic (Wilson interval for the yes-share, delta method for past-due/principal, no finite-population correction). They show how far the rates could move on future traffic, not an error in this cohort's own numbers. Blue = stored autogluon_v25; rust = Full package 1 MSE, the BASELINE of this dashboard: the architecture of the productive model (a simple AutoGluon MSE) retrained on the updated union dataset, which every other Full model tries to improve on; charcoal = selected Full challenger. Bottom: union holdout, delinquency = 1 - pngi_rate.",
  "note_html": "\n<p><strong>Top row (production, BNPL only):</strong> stored <code>autogluon_v25</code> from\n<code>pre_scored_users</code>, the locally scored Full package 1 MSE (the productive architecture, a simple AutoGluon MSE, retrained on the union dataset: the baseline of this dashboard) on the same users\n(these two can differ), and the selected challenger. The cohort is every last-3-month BNPL <code>bnpl.api_requests</code> user with a stored v25: a census, not a sample, so the cohort's own rates carry no sampling error. Delinquency = the whole matured BNPL CaaS book of the last 180 days (every user with a matured installment, completed_plans &gt; 0 and a stored v25): also a census.</p>\n<p>The shaded bands and the \"(95% CI)\" figures treat each cohort as one draw from ongoing traffic (Wilson interval for the yes-share, delta method for past-due/principal, no finite-population correction). They show how far the rates could move on future traffic, not an error in this cohort's own numbers.</p>\n<p><strong>Bottom row (holdout):</strong> gated yes-share of union <code>test_v25.csv</code>\n(BNPL leftover + cards, same 80/20 split as prior v25). Delinquency = labeled unpaid share\n<code>1 − pngi_rate</code> in the score tail — not Fritz CaaS pesos.\nThe original Fritz Pages copy is unchanged.</p>\n<p><strong>Holdout users left out:</strong> 251 of the 2,133 holdout users have no <code>geolocation_access</code> value, so they cannot pass the geolocation gate. They are excluded from both holdout curves (the bottom row uses the other 1,882). The live top row follows the Fritz SQL instead: <code>IFNULL(..., 0)</code>, a missing gate counts as a failure and the user stays in the denominator. Under that rule the baseline's holdout yes-share at 0.65 would be 37.4% instead of the 42.4% drawn. Each holdout variant states its own figures under the charts; the curves are not changed.</p>",
